course: dictionary
lesson: 9804
title: Swahili, a shared language
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Swahili is a language spoken by many millions of people in East Africa, especially in Kenya, Tanzania and Uganda. In Swahili itself it is called Kiswahili. People who speak different first languages use it to trade and talk, so it is a shared language, a lingua franca.
explain: Some Swahili words have travelled into English. «Safari» means journey, «jambo» means hello, and «Hakuna matata» means no worries, from The Lion King. «Asante» means thank you. When you meet a word you do not know, read the sentence around it for clues.
explain: Swahili is also the name of a people of the East African coast, and it describes their culture, such as Swahili cities and Swahili food. So the word can name a language, a people, or describe things, like «Swahili words».
fact: Swahili is written in the Latin alphabet.
fact: «Simba» means lion in Swahili.
fact: «Asante» means thank you.
fact: Swahili changes the start of a word: «mtoto» (child) becomes «watoto» (children).
fact: Swahili is a shared language for well over 100 million people in East Africa.
exercise: In The Lion King, «simba» is used for a young lion. What does simba mean in Swahili? | Hello | King | Journey | *Lion
exercise: «Swahili cities like Kilwa and Mombasa grew rich.» What does the word like tell you? | Kilwa is a lion | *Kilwa and Mombasa are examples of Swahili cities | Kilwa and Mombasa are the only Swahili cities | Swahili cities are not rich
exercise: Which word means thank you in Swahili? | *Asante | Simba | Jambo | Safari
test: What is Swahili, in the first lesson sense? | A ship from India | A type of food only | *A language spoken by many people in East Africa | A lion in a film
test: «Mtu» means person, and «watu» means people. What does «mtoto» mean? | Children | Lion | Person | *Child
exam: A lingua franca is a shared language used between people who speak different first languages. Which is an example? | *Swahili in East Africa | Latin letters in English books | Simba the lion | Hakuna matata on its own
